WEBVTT

0
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:04.679
Welcome back.

1
00:00:05.019 --> 00:00:06.260
In the last video,

2
00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.399
we dismantle the illusion of the independent

3
00:00:09.699 --> 00:00:10.880
stateless node.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.810
Distributed AI systems are deeply stateful,

5
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.760
but what does maintaining state actually mean

6
00:00:18.319 --> 00:00:20.420
across 10,000 GPUs?

7
00:00:21.129 --> 00:00:23.049
In a traditional application,

8
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.209
state usually means saving user data to a database.

9
00:00:28.139 --> 00:00:29.879
But in distributed AI

10
00:00:30.319 --> 00:00:30.819
state

11
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.500
refers to the billions of mathematical weights that make up the neural network.

12
00:00:36.729 --> 00:00:36.740
Because

13
00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:40.009
the model is too massive to fit on a single chip.

14
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.689
It is fractured across thousands of GPUs

15
00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:47.860
that must behave as a coordinated system.

16
00:00:48.439 --> 00:00:52.159
This brings us to the heaviest burden in AI infrastructure,

17
00:00:52.319 --> 00:00:53.799
the coordination tax.

18
00:00:54.619 --> 00:00:58.000
Every time those GPUs compute a new batch of data,

19
00:00:58.419 --> 00:00:59.479
they must stop,

20
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.330
communicate their results across the network,

21
00:01:02.599 --> 00:01:04.290
average out the new parameters,

22
00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:07.480
and coordinate before progressing to the next step.

23
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.120
At scale,

24
00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:11.589
coordination becomes infrastructure.

25
00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:16.080
Think about the sheer physics of that coordination.

26
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.580
You're forcing thousands of independent machines

27
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.800
to share gigabytes of data in a tightly coordinated manner.

28
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.199
If coordination across the network slows down,

29
00:01:27.559 --> 00:01:30.029
the coordination tax skyrockets,

30
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.980
and your compute efficiency

31
00:01:32.279 --> 00:01:33.959
drops significantly.

32
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:38.029
But what happens when something inevitably goes wrong?

33
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:38.809
Because

34
00:01:39.519 --> 00:01:42.720
distributed AI systems are so tightly coupled,

35
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.300
the blast radius of a failure is very different from traditional systems.

36
00:01:48.879 --> 00:01:49.900
We call this

37
00:01:50.279 --> 00:01:51.599
failure propagation.

38
00:01:52.309 --> 00:01:53.739
In a standard cloud,

39
00:01:53.959 --> 00:01:56.970
a degraded node is an isolated incident.

40
00:01:57.269 --> 00:01:58.199
In AI,

41
00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.309
if just one GPU out of thousands experiences a

42
00:02:02.309 --> 00:02:05.980
thermal throttle and slows down by a few milliseconds,

43
00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.050
it becomes a straggler because the system

44
00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:10.419
is synchronous,

45
00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.210
the rest of the system may be forced to wait.

46
00:02:13.589 --> 00:02:14.889
They sit idle.

47
00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:20.910
That single degraded node can introduce cascading delays across the cluster.

48
00:02:22.089 --> 00:02:24.110
And if a node outright fails,

49
00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.350
the current training step may need to be retried.

50
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.039
The system halts,

51
00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.119
discards the current step,

52
00:02:32.399 --> 00:02:37.490
and may require rolling portions of the workload back to the last saved checkpoint.

53
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.460
Introducing significant recovery overhead

54
00:02:41.839 --> 00:02:43.600
and lost compute capacity.

55
00:02:45.009 --> 00:02:48.039
You are no longer managing independent servers,

56
00:02:48.289 --> 00:02:50.850
you are managing a tightly coordinated,

57
00:02:50.889 --> 00:02:52.070
distributed system

58
00:02:52.449 --> 00:02:53.949
where small disruptions

59
00:02:54.089 --> 00:02:57.529
can affect behavior across the entire environment.